# A typed error has to survive the wire

Carl, 2026-08-25: "We need to build some kind of conversion that
converts exceptions to something that can travel over gRPC."

## Where it stands

In-process, a Nix failure is now typed and clean: `BadStorePathName`
under `BadStorePath` under `NixError`, with `str(e)` stripped and
`e.colored` holding what libstore wrote (tasks/015).

None of that survives a round trip. The path an error takes today:

- the server catches anything that is not a `WrapperError` and wraps
  it in `InternalError(message, cause=e)`;
- `InternalError.to_dict()` writes `cause_type` (the class NAME) and
  `cause_message` into JSON, which crosses in the gRPC status;
- `WrapperError.from_dict()` rebuilds the cause by looking that name
  up in a hard-coded map of five builtins - ValueError, TypeError,
  RuntimeError, KeyError, OSError - and falls back to `Exception`.

So a `BadStorePath` arrives as a plain `Exception` carrying the right
message and the wrong type, and `except BadStorePath` on the client
catches nothing. `e.colored` is gone entirely.

## What it is now

Both halves changed, because the first one made the second obvious.

**The hierarchy is declared, not listed.** `cythonix_bindings/__init__.py`
declares `_errors_module`; the codegen reflects it and writes the class
names, their bases and their `_wire_fields` into the manifest. Adding a
Nix error means adding it to `errors.py` and nothing else. A build-time
check builds one of each from its declared parts and reads them back,
so a class whose `__init__` reorders or renames a part fails the BUILD
rather than the first remote failure.

**A failure travels as messages.** Carl: "You're saying that we should
use JSON inside protobuf? Sounds like an antipattern to me."

He was right, and the answer was not to defend the JSON. It was not
inside a protobuf message - a failed unary call carries no message at
all - it was in the status MESSAGE, the human-readable field. Structure
in a text field is the wrong shape, and gRPC has the right one:
`grpc-status-details-bin`, a `google.rpc.Status` whose `details` is a
repeated `Any`.

So `grpc_schema` emits a `Fault` message plus one message per declared
error class, from the same manifest table. A failure sends the `Fault`
always, and the cause as its OWN message when the bindings declare its
class. The status message goes back to being what a human reads.

That last part is what makes the third direction better than the
second, which this file used to rank below it. The `Any`'s type name is
the class identity, and the far side resolves it in the schema pool or
not at all. There is no name to decide whether it is safe to construct,
because no class name crosses on its own.

`_runtime.from_dict` is gone. It was the map of five builtins, in the
one module emitted beside the wrappers that must not know which library
it wraps.

## What is left

One hand-written list survives, in `faults.py`: the builtins a cause is
approximated as when nothing declares it - ValueError, TypeError,
RuntimeError, KeyError, OSError. It is not duplicated binding
knowledge. Most of it is this layer's OWN vocabulary: the lifecycle
core raises KeyError for an unknown handle, and no binding is involved.

The part that IS binding knowledge is Cython's automatic mapping -
`std::invalid_argument` surfaces as ValueError with nothing declaring
it. The way to remove that is to declare what a binding raises, not to
grow the map. Until then the approximation is confined to the case it
is actually for, and `test_contracts` holds the line: no layer above
the bindings may name a DECLARED error class.
